<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  network_security_config.xml (v1.4.1 security review)

  WHY THIS EXISTS
  ===============
  The manifest already sets android:usesCleartextTraffic="false", but that
  attribute is a single global switch: on some OEM Android 8/9 builds it is
  inconsistently honoured for certain HTTP stacks, and it cannot express
  per-domain intent. This config makes the policy explicit, auditable and
  forward-compatible:

    1. BASELINE: cleartext HTTP is denied for EVERY destination — including
       user-added CAs (traffic must be TLS with system-trusted certs only).
    2. No domain is ever exempted. The app talks to:
         - Firebase Auth / Firestore / FCM  (Google-operated, TLS-only,
           cert handling owned by Google Play services — GMS traffic is
           already validated by the platform, custom pinning there breaks
           Play-services updates and is explicitly NOT recommended)
         - R2 presigned PUT/GET endpoints
           (https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com — TLS-only, signed,
           short-lived URLs; the URL itself carries the authorization, so
           there is nothing extra to pin)

  CERTIFICATE PINNING EVALUATION (decision: NOT ADDED, documented)
  ----------------------------------------------------------------
  Pinning was evaluated for the three traffic classes and rejected:
    * Firebase/GMS: pinned certs are rotated by Google; a static pin causes
      hard outages. Google documents pinning as unsupported for Play
      services traffic.
    * R2/cloudflarestorage.com: Cloudflare rotates leaf certs; a pin would
      break uploads on rotation. Authorization here is the presigned URL,
      minted server-side with ≤10-minute lifetime — replay risk is bounded
      by the signature, not by pinning.
    * Custom endpoints: none exist. If a first-party API is ever added,
      pin its leaf + backup key there via pin-set and update this file.

  If a future build NEEDS pinning (e.g. self-hosted TURN/API), add a
  <pin-set> scoped to that exact domain — never a global pin.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- System CAs ONLY: user-installed certificates cannot be used
                 to MITM the app (anti-tamper hardening). -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
